Figure 1. (a) Illustration of the crystal structure of SCU-13. Color code: Pb (green), Cl (blue), benzene ring (black). (b) Illustration of the π-π stacking mode along the a axis. (c) and (d) The visualization electronic orbitals of SCU-13. Valence band (VB) edge is localized at C1 sites and conduction band (CB) edge is from the empty π orbitals of C2 and C3 sites. Grey, C; red, O; green, Cl; cyan, Pb.

Single crystal X-ray diffraction analysis shows that SCU-13 crystallizes in the tetragonal space group, \(14_{1}/\mathsf{a}\), as dark brown crystals (Table S1). The pure phase was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (Figure S1). The overall structure can be described as a porous 3D anionic framework with two interpenetrating channels \((\sim\!12\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\,\times\,4\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}})\) along a and b axes, respectively (Figure 1a). The protonated dimethylamine serves as a counter ion located in the channels of SCU-13. Each \(\mathsf{P b}^{2+}\) is coordinated by eight oxygen atoms from four ligands forming a dodecahedral molecular geometry. Interestingly, semiquinoid ligands adopt two types of π-π stacking modes, where one is parallel to the a axis and the other is parallel to the b axis. The shortest distance between two neighboring benzene rings is around \(3.6\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) (Figure 1b). This stacked structure would promote the ligand orbital overlap, enhancing the charge mobility within the [ab] plane.

A pronounced absorption feature from 550 to \(700\;\mathsf{n m}\) in UVvis absorption spectrum (Figure S2) demonstrates that the semicondutive nature of SCU-13 is predominately determined by the organic ligand. The calculated energy gap for SCU-13 is \(1.74\,\mathsf{e V}\), one of benchmark values for semiconductor detection materials. To elucidate the semiconductive properties and charge mobility behavior of SCU-13, the band structure was calculated using density functional theory. Figure S3 indicates SCU-13 is an indirect semiconductor. The effective masses of the hole and the electron were computed to be \(3.30\;\mathsf{m}_{0}\) and \(1.03\;\mathsf{m}_{0}\), respectively, implying that the electron mobility is notably

higher than that of the hole. The orbital analysis demonstrates that the valence band edge is localized at C1 sites, and the conduction band edge is from the empty π orbitals of C2 and C3 sites (Figure 1c and 1d). Overall, these findings confirm that organic ligands provide efficient charge transport pathways within the [ab] plane, suggesting the anisotropic conductive nature.

Thermoplastical method was employed to fabricate a large-area semiconductive membrane with \(4\times4\;\mathsf{c m}^{2}\). Figure 2a displays the optimal membrane has excellent flexibility with a loading ratio of \(50\%\). Powder X-ray diffraction data indicates the structural integrity of SCU-13 during the thermoplastical process (Figure S1). The dark brown coloration of membrane initially implies the semiconductive nature of the membrane. In addition, we estimated the X-ray attenuation efficiency of membrane, as shown in Figure 2b, showing advantages over the most commercially utilized materials of Si and α-Se.

