To fully understand the electrical properties of the semiconductive membrane, we compared the I-V characteristics of membrane-based detector and pelleted MOF based detector. The resistivity of membrane derived from the I-V curve is \(6.98\times10^{10}\;\Omega\cdot\mathsf{c m}\), which is only \(32\,\%\) of that of pristine SCU-13 (Figure
S4). This discrepancy was further supported by a scanning electron microscopy topographical analysis. Figure S5 displays a homogeneous distribution of MOFs in membrane, which significantly reduces the grain boundary resistance. It should be noted that the resistivity of membrane is a critical parameter for the evaluation of direct X-ray-to-charge conversion. Furthermore, we quantitatively tracked the difference in trap density \((\mathsf{n}_{\mathsf{t r a p}})\) between membrane-based detector and pelleted MOF based detectors by space charge-limited currents method (Figure S6 and S7). With the increase of bias voltage, the I-V curves climb from the linear ohmic region, through the trap-filled limited region, and finally reach the Child's region. The \(\mathsf{n}_{\mathsf{t r a p}}\) of the membrane is estimated to be \(1.74\times10^{10}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) , lower than that of the pelleted sample \((6.09\times10^{10}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) . These observations demonstrate that membrane-based detector exhibits a higher charge mobility than that of the pelleted MOF based detector.

Moreover, the charge carrier mobility and lifetime product \((\upmu\up T)\) is a crucial figure of merit for evaluating the charge mobility length in \(\mathsf{X}\) -ray detectors.[18] Not surprisingly, the \(\upmu\up T\) of the membrane-based detector turns out to be \(4.31\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\) , higher than that of the pelleted based detector \((3.76\,\times\,10^{-4}\) \(\mathsf{c m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{V}^{-1})\) , implying another merit by doping MOFs into mixed matrix membrane (Figure 2c). Notably, this value is a recordhigh value reported for all MOF based detectors, comparable with the emerging materials (the organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite: \(10^{-7}~\sim~10^{-3}~\mathsf{c m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{V}^{-1})^{[19]}\) as well as commercial materials CZT and \(\mathsf{P b l}_{2}\) \((\sim\,3\,\times\,10^{-3}\,\mathsf{\ c m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\) and \(1\,\times\,10^{-5}\) \(\mathsf{c m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\) [20]

Figures 2d presents the typical I-V curves of the membranebased detector ranging from -10 to \(+10\,\mathrm{~V~}\) under the X-ray irradiation with different received dose rates. Clearly, the I-V curve exhibits a linear dependence, confirming the charge carrier effectively generated under X-ray irradiation. We further monitored the photocurrent change under different X-ray dose rate irradiation. As shown in Figure 2e, high dose rate X-ray significantly enhance the concentrations of charge carrier, leading a high photocurrent. In addition, we investigated radiation stability of membrane based detector. Only \(2.0\ \%\) reduction of photocurrent was observed after long time irradiation, demonstrating an excellent stability under working conditions (Figure S8). I-t curves in Figure 2f were recorded by switching X-ray on and off, showing excellent operational stability under different bias voltage. Notably, the photocurrent displays a sharp increase upon X-ray irradiation with on-off ratio reaching up to 900 under \(\textsf{1V}\) bias voltage, significantly larger than those of reported flexible detectors (Table S3).[7]

