The detection sensitivity, defined as \(\mathsf{S}=\partial\mathsf{J}\) /2D (where J is the current density, D is dose rate), is an important parameter to evaluate detection performance. Figure 3a shows the relationship between current density and received dose rate. Fitting the curve affords a highest sensitivity of \(65.86~\upmu\mathsf{C}\mathsf{y}_{\mathsf{a i r}}\) \(\mathsf{1c m}^{-2}\) under a constant bias of \(100\,\mathrm{V}\) . The detection performance of the membrane is notably better than the pelleted sample, whose sensitivity is lower (Table S2). In addition, a breakdown occurs when the applied bias is larger than \(50\,\mathrm{V}\) for the pelleted sample. The obtained detection sensitivity of membrane-based detector is also superior to those of reported flexible X-ray detectors and some perovskite devices, such as \(\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}\) perovskite films,[21] \(\mathsf{G a O}_{3}\) thin films,[22] and \((\mathsf{B A})_{2}\mathsf{C s A g B i B r}_{7}\) single crystal (Table S3).[9] It is worth mentioning that this value is on the same level with those of the commercial \(\mathfrak{a}\) -Se detector and Cd(Zn)Te single crystal.[11,123] Figure 3b shows the signal-tonoise ratio (SNR) under different received dose rates with several bias voltages. The lowest dose rate is \(6.553~\upmu\mathsf{G}\mathsf{y}_{\mathsf{a i r}}/\mathsf{\hat{s}}\) , lower than the standard detection limit requirement for medical imaging.[24]

